+\reply
+We have added a source-to-target result and state equivalence study in Section~IV-D. We randomly sampled 100 MySQL test cases and evaluated SQLGlot, RISE, \sedar{}, and \tool{} on the same cases for transfer to DuckDB. We compared query results and the resulting database states, including schemas and table contents. Translation or execution failures count as non-equivalent, and both rates use all 100 cases as the denominator. \tool{} achieves 52.0\% result equivalence and 74.0\% state equivalence, compared with 14.0\%/56.0\% for SQLGlot, 34.0\%/65.0\% for RISE, and 38.0\%/69.0\% for \sedar{}. This study provides supplementary evidence for the sampled source-target pair, without claiming general semantic equivalence. We have also renamed ``Semantic Correctness'' to ``Execution Validity'' throughout the paper and clarified its definition in Section~IV-A to distinguish runtime executability from result or state equivalence.
+
